Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:06.150 align:start position:0%
 
first off congratulations i absolutely  adore your movie  adele my first question is for you was  writing for the animated feature format  always a goal of yours or did this  opportunity kind of come out of the blue  it came out of the clear blue sky and i  am so excited that it did  you know i grew up in malaysia i grew up  watching disney movies and i don't think  it was in my wildest dreams that i  thought i'd be  you know helping to write um on one  but the idea that i was going to be part  of creating you know the next disney  warrior princess and have the culture be  inspired by where i grew up like that's  crazy um so anyway um the eight-year-old  in me would have just shrieked with  delight  and would be so proud of you now i still  can't get over this story  what would you say was the biggest  lesson learned or maybe the most  challenging pivot to make when it comes  from going from  writing for live action to writing for  animation  you know strangely because most of my  career it was as a tv writer  it actually was the perfect sort of  transition and fit i think for uh  feature writers  you know you have this idea of future  writers you know on their laptops by  themselves at a coffee shop  and that could not be further from the  truth when it comes to feature animation  it is incredibly collaborative  so instead of going into a room with a  big table surrounded by writers i'm  surrounded by  the most talented story artists in the  world so when even when it came to  breaking story you know and you're  we have all these pictures of like like  thousands of cards pinned up on the wall  you know coming up with like what the  big themes of the story are who the  individual characters are what their  arts are going to be  i had all these teammates in the story  department and even in the writing of it  um you know i'd write a scene and then  you know fawn versus thor would take it  and you know put in all these amazing  visual  ideas and dynamics and even lines  because these story artists um what i  hadn't realized they're cinematographers  and directors and writers  um so being able to be inspired by their  boards i would write to that and then  they would write to my script so it is  this sort of cyclical  like you know process of inspiration um  you know that is that is tremendous  that you don't have to do it alone as a  writer thank god  so oh it's not now i believe this is  your second feature  as a full producer on the production so  is there any  tool in your producing toolkit so to  speak that you would  credit with having worked on moana that  you're now applying to  raya yeah i think there are quite a few  actually one of the most important  things i think though was um  discovering a way that worked for us to  work with  um experts cultural experts from the  region with the oceanic story trust on  moana  i'm still in touch with almost everyone  on it because these great friendships  for  and so we we applied that on with our  southeast asia story trust as well we  had a  a visual cultural anthropologist which  is so interesting this sort of expertise  is the visual representation of anything  within culture he's lao  but he's his knowledge is throughout the  region and we had so many experts like  that that joined us along the way  i think um there are there are a million  tools though because  the truth is that um you really do have  to trust the process when it's a process  this long  it sometimes feels like you're going  backwards we try something we push  against some idea  and then we do a screening with our with  our story trust and oops  we went too far in one direction or  another you have to trust that we are  always  getting getting there making something  better and better and better and once  you've done it once you can trust it  that much more easily  was there any point in this process  where there was a new idea being  implemented and you're like i don't know  about this i don't know if it's going to  work and then all of a sudden later on  it clicked and you saw that that was the  right move for the story and the  characters  that happens so often in all parts of  the story process because we really we  put it up we break it all down  and sometimes you get attached to an old  idea and you know when a brave new idea  is put forth i think a great example is  you know to give credit to don hall um  this idea of the the ending and what  that act of trust  um you know is going to look like in the  climax of the movie  and we you know there are many stories  about you know pulling people together  for a common goal  but if the if the if the underlying  message is about trust how do we show  that in the last  movie it's not about being the biggest  strongest warrior in the room you know  ryan  namara could easily be that but it is  this uh ultimate act of sacrifice and  showing that you  are this person who is worthy of being  trusted  um you know and rai who has no reason to  trust anyone  after being betrayed so you know so much  she still finds the strength to reach  out because she is the hero of that  movie  um i think it was the idea of that and  then you know um having the  incredibly talented uh john rip our  co-director you know put  up those boards and we all saw and  immediately were struck by the  emotionality of that moment  uh you know i think that that was the  the prime example of like having a very  hard to work around concept  intellectually  and then seeing it realize and seeing  that it worked better than anything you  could have thought of  i think for me it's um it's less  something that i didn't think would work  it's more something that i didn't think  we'd get away with  which was um having three strong  interesting  nuanced female leads and  exploring at the very heart of a a major  feature a friendship between two female  characters and  and even that opposition kind of  friendship relationship that ray has  with namari  so i um nobody ever said no and so it's  something that we pushed on and and  um adele and in particular could not be  more excited about  just a last act to add on to that which  was i think we you know because austin  and i got so excited about you know  creating these characters at one point  we actually looked up and realized like  oh all our characters are female  including tong  you know started as a female character  and i never thought we'd ever be in this  position of thinking well maybe  you know for equity we need to like uh  you know have  one more male character what was it like  formulating that group of heroes that we  see  together throughout the movie was was  there any kind of mixing and matching  along the way adding and subtracting  anything like that  yeah there was a young baby noise she  started off as  uh actually tong was a grandmother um  has the grandchild and they were  together she was a warrior a warrior  baby she already had that  tough thing that we love so much about  her and we realized as we looked at it  that  at that point we had the ongi's where  they were the main the monkey kind of  characters  the main characters from from uh talon  and we wanted a human  character there and came up with the  idea of how she works with them  um boone was somewhere else to begin  with and  we've moved him they evolved and we  thought of him as you know the  the han solo he's the he's the getaway  the getaway car  is our is our emporium and you know all  those parallels the way we talk  and shorthand with each other but it was  super fun to come up with them and they  we did and when they landed when  we and especially when a voice actor  would come in like  benedict ron came in and and and and  brought tong to life and you knew okay  we're really on to we're on to something  pitch perfect casting  i did have one off topic question for  you because  what you did on the crazy rich asian  sequel is  i imagine a very scary thing to do but  it's also a very important stand to make  so for any  other writer out there who might run  into a similar situation given the fact  that you've already gone through it  what would you advise them to do you  know i think you can only speak to your  truth  um you know with what crazy rich and  similar to ryan it has done so much for  asian representation  and you know you want to be able to have  people uh behind the scenes as well  you know telling those stories there's  um you know it all comes down to  um saying we have for underrepresented  communities that know stories about us  without us  and so you know i think disney's great  credit um it's not just me  and my co-writer qui gwen but we had so  many um people um from southeast asia or  had southeast asian cultural roots  already in the disney family with our  you know our story department visual  development um you know uh  uh in animation we're able to give that  layer of authenticity and you know  dedication and love to the project  and so really that's what you know  that's what you love to see and  hopefully you know  all the whole industry is you know  striving towards a  more diverse and uh accurate reflection  of the world we live in today  with faces in front of and behind the  screen i appreciate you sharing that  thank you both so much for your time  today my one and a half year old niece  is obsessed with moana and i am  determined to make raya next on that  list  i love it

